VERDICT: UPHOLD
CHECKER: judge-standards
FAMILY: adversarial
TASK: WS5
ATTEMPT: 4
---
PREMISE CHECK (mechanical, independent of checker-second's own run)

Reproduced twice against the WS5.4 snapshot
(scratch/snap/WS5.4-second, `.swarm/diffs/WS5.4.diff` applied):

1. Isolated regex probe (scratch/judges/WS5.4-standards/repro.go):
   fmt.Errorf("persons: invalid birth_month for %q: %w", `Robert "Bob" Smith`, ...)
   -> msg = `persons: invalid birth_month for "Robert \"Bob\" Smith": month is required`
   rePersonsInvalidBirthMonth (`^persons: invalid birth_month for "([^"]*)": .+$`)
   .FindStringSubmatch(msg) -> nil (NO MATCH).

2. Direct call into the snapshot's own personsSaveErrorMessage (temp test
   file, removed after use; nice -n 19, GOMAXPROCS=4, GOFLAGS="-count=1
   -p=2"):
   name=`Robert "Bob" Smith`, submitted BirthMonth="" (the EMPTY class) ->
   `go test ./internal/handlers/whatif/ -run TestJudgeProbeQuotedNameEmptyMonth -v`
   -> msg = `Invalid person data: persons: invalid birth_month for
   "Robert \"Bob\" Smith": month is required` -- NOT
   `Robert "Bob" Smith needs a birth month`.

This confirms checker-second's factual premise exactly: for a person Name
containing a literal `"`, Go's `%q` escapes it as `\"`, the regex's
`[^"]*` capture cannot span the escape, FindStringSubmatch returns nil, and
personsSaveErrorMessage falls through to the generic
`"Invalid person data: " + msg` branch -- for BOTH the empty and the
unparseable birth-month classes, since both share this same capture. The
name field (`person_name[]`) carries no `pattern`/quote restriction in
rate-assumptions.html or whatif-rate-assumptions.js (confirmed independently
by both checker-second and checker-a11y's markup review), so this is an
ordinary, reachable input, not a contrived one. checker-tests independently
reproduced the identical bug as its own F2 finding (V03m/V03n/V03o) and
chose not to fail on it -- a disagreement of JUDGMENT between two checkers
about the SAME confirmed fact, which is exactly what the dispute process
exists to settle. Premise: TRUE.

CLAUSE-BY-CLAUSE, against ruling g's text as written

Ruling g enumerates five classes with deliberately asymmetric drafting:
  1. submitted birth month EMPTY -> "<Name> needs a birth month" -- NO
     "or equivalent" hedge.
  2. present but unparseable -> "<Name>'s birth month "<value>" isn't a
     valid month" (or equivalent -- must name the person and must not say
     it is missing).
  3. birth month after start -> the attempt-3 message (unchanged, not at
     issue here).
  4. invalid projection start date -> a message about the START DATE
     (never "Invalid person data").
  5. other persons errors stay truthful (no specific wording).

The governing clause for a given 400 is fixed by the SUBMITTED VALUE
("Word every ... 400 from the SUBMITTED value"), not by which code branch
happens to fire. A quoted name with an EMPTY submitted birth month is
squarely class 1's fact pattern; it does not become class 5 ("other persons
errors") merely because a regex bug misroutes it there. The inclusion of an
explicit "(or equivalent ...)" parenthetical on class 2 and its deliberate
absence from class 1 and 3 is textually meaningful (expressio unius) --
class 1's wording is the strict one of the set.

Applying clause 1 to the reproduced case: required text is exactly
`Robert "Bob" Smith needs a birth month`. Actual text is `Invalid person
data: persons: invalid birth_month for "Robert \"Bob\" Smith": month is
required` -- the internal validator's field name ("invalid birth_month
for"), Go's raw wrapped error text ("month is required"), and a
backslash-mangled re-escaping of the name are all exposed verbatim to the
end user. This is not a paraphrase of the required wording under any
reading, strict or lenient; it also reproduces the literal phrase "Invalid
person data" that ruling g singles out by name as the thing to avoid (for
the start-date class explicitly, but substantively for exactly this
species of raw-error leakage the whole attempt-4 contract exists to end --
see 2026-09-24f, which flagged this exact fallback text as the defect that
forced attempt 4). Clause 1, as written, is not met for this input.

(For the present-but-unparseable class with the same quoted name,
class 2's "or equivalent" hedge is arguably met -- the escaped name and
value are technically present in the fallback text and it never says
"missing" -- so that half of checker-second's F2 twin would be closer to
the line. That is not what carries the FAIL: the EMPTY-birth-month +
quoted-name case, governed by the unhedged clause 1, is clearly outside
the written standard, and checker-second's own repro is exactly that
case.)

Reachability objection considered and rejected: checker-tests notes the
EMPTY-birth-month class is only reachable via direct POST because the UI
field is `required` -- true, but that is equally true of the ALREADY-
ACCEPTED P3d oracle check for plain names, which the run treats as
squarely in scope (2026-09-24e/g; oracle P3d exists for exactly this
class). A direct-POST-only reachability does not place this input outside
the standard; it places it exactly where the standard's own accepted test
already probes.

"Invalid person data" is explicitly forbidden ONLY for the start-date
class -- correct as a matter of the literal negative instruction. But
ruling g does not need a second explicit prohibition to fail here: clause
1's own affirmative, unhedged wording requirement is sufficient on its own,
and the observed text fails to satisfy it regardless of whether "Invalid
person data" is separately banned for this class.

RULING

The contested FAIL's premise is verified true by independent reproduction
at both the regex level and the handler-function level. Read against
ruling g's actual asymmetric wording -- no hedge on the empty-birth-month
clause, an explicit hedge only on the unparseable clause -- the quoted-name
input governed by the unhedged clause fails the letter of the standard: a
realistic, browser-reachable (given the accepted P3d precedent for
reachability) name defeats the new regex and produces raw internal
validator text instead of the required "<Name> needs a birth month"
wording. The lead's "substance is truthfulness, only polish is missing"
reading asks this lens to excuse a clause the ruling's own drafters chose
not to hedge; that is not available under a standards/letter reading.
UPHOLD.
